A binding gives a value a visible name.

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    count = 42
    ratio F64 = 0.5
]

### Declaration forms

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    name = value
    name Type = value
]

- A binding must have an initializer.
- Without an annotation, the initializer supplies its shape and numeric-literal family. An eligible numeric origin may stay unresolved until body-wide constraints select its exact type.
- An explicit annotation supplies an exact receiving type. Raw literals can materialise directly in it, while already typed values need exact compatibility.
- Ordinary bindings are immutable by default.
- `=` creates a binding when the name is new.
- `=` reassigns an existing binding only when that binding is mutable.

### Numeric inference in a runtime body

One function body, including nested blocks, branches, loops and handlers, is
one numeric inference boundary. The implicit root runtime body follows the
same rule. Eligible exact annotations, assignment targets, concrete parameter,
return and field receivers, copies and mutable writes all constrain numeric
origins before fallback. Every eligible use participates regardless of source
order, and each binding keeps one static type across all paths.

After eligible body-wide constraints resolve numeric origins, an otherwise
unconstrained whole-number origin defaults to `I32`; a decimal/exponent origin
defaults to `F64`. These defaults do not set a minimum arithmetic width. A bound
integer keeps its family, and a result constraint cannot infer named arithmetic
operands backwards. See @../numbers (Numbers) for the exact numeric rules.

Compile-time constants use `#` and are documented separately by @../constants (Constants and compile-time behaviour).

### Binding mode and type

Binding mode is not semantic type identity.

An immutable `String` binding and a mutable `String` binding still have the
same semantic `String` type. The binding decides which later operations are
permitted.

### Scope

A binding is visible in its scope and child scopes unless a language rule
creates a narrower visibility boundary.

Moth does not allow a visible name to be redeclared. See the Shadowing
section for the exact rule.
